window.docContents[13].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Mean-Value-Formulas":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity","header":"5.3.2. Mean-Value Formulas","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Mean-Value-Formulas"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations","header":"7.2. Transport and Classical Waves","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation":{"contents":"\n\nEvans §7.2.1–2 (pp. 398–406) defines weak solutions of the second-order\nhyperbolic Dirichlet problem, constructs finite-dimensional Galerkin\napproximations, proves their energy bounds, and passes to a weak limit. We work\nover a Gelfand triple , and the\nexistence theorem takes as hypothesis a bounded projected Galerkin family whose\nstable trial projections converge strongly to the identity on .\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution","header":"7.3.1. Weak Waves by Galerkin Approximation","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Shared-analysis":{"contents":"Mathlib supplies a common mathematical language as well as individual theorems.\nIts account of a unified, community-maintained library emphasizes the hierarchy\nof structures and the organization that makes results reusable\n. For PDE work, using the same integrals,\nderivatives, function spaces, and continuous linear maps lets an estimate connect\nto existing analysis. PDEAtlas builds on those definitions. Compatibility also\nrequires decisions about domains, boundary conditions, and solution concepts:\ntwo theorems called “Sobolev embedding” need not have interchangeable hypotheses.\n\nTerence Tao's Lean companion to Analysis I makes this transition explicit\n. Its commentary refers readers to the book\nand documents changes required for Mathlib compatibility. Chapter 2 develops a\nseparate natural-number type; an epilogue introduces its correspondence with\nMathlib's natural numbers before later chapters use the shared library.\nExercises deliberately retain sorry placeholders, including some of those\ncorrespondence proofs. PDE libraries need analogous identifications: for a\nDirichlet problem, identifying the closure of\ncompactly supported smooth functions in a Sobolev norm with the space of\nfunctions having zero boundary trace lets an estimate stated in one formulation\napply in the other.\n\nThe July 2024 Zulip discussion that helped start PDEAtlas\n pointed to substantial analysis already\nin Mathlib. Turning those foundations into PDE proofs required further work.\n\nTau Ceti explores another way to extend that shared library. Its human-written\nroadmaps direct AI contributors, with adversarial review aimed at reusable\nmathematics; the project explicitly defers to Mathlib's design decisions\n. Its PDE roadmap organizes the analysis needed for elliptic and parabolic\nresearch into six dependency lanes .\nA ✓ marks implemented coverage of the named area, under the hypotheses of\nPDEAtlas's theorems. It is not a claim that every milestone or every domain\nregularity in that lane is covered. “Compare” marks a lane whose full scope\nstill needs a statement-level comparison.\n\n* Area* PDEAtlas coverage* Corresponding PDEAtlas development\n* A: domain function spaces* ✓* Weak-derivative Sobolev spaces, density, embeddings, traces, extension and compactness in\n    PDE/Spaces/Sobolev/.\n* B: harmonic-analysis estimates* Compare* PDE/Analysis/ contains reusable estimate infrastructure; the full proposed\n    singular-integral and interpolation program needs a separate statement-level comparison.\n* C: maximum principles and potential theory* ✓* Classical harmonic analysis and ball/half-space kernels in PDE/Elliptic/Classical/ and\n    PotentialTheory/.\n* D: linear elliptic existence* ✓* Weak Dirichlet problems, Fredholm alternatives and spectral constructions in\n    PDE/Elliptic/Variational/.\n* E: elliptic regularity* ✓* Interior, boundary and higher-order results in PDE/Elliptic/Regularity/, with coefficient\n    and boundary hypotheses in each theorem.\n* F: parabolic evolution* ✓* Weak evolution, heat constructions, energy estimates and Dirichlet semigroups in\n    PDE/Analysis/Evolution/ and PDE/Parabolic/.\n* Symbolic calculus and certified numerics* ✓* The CAS and CEGA chapter: normalization,\n    differentiation, supported integration, interval bounds and certificate APIs.\n* Hyperbolic equations and fluid mechanics* ✓* Wave and transport theory in PDE/Hyperbolic/, and Euler, Navier–Stokes\n    and Stokes constructions in PDE/Fluid/.\n\nThe closest overlap is the domain Sobolev and energy-method infrastructure.\nA whole-space Fourier Sobolev space and a domain weak-derivative space\nrequire an agreement theorem in the appropriate exponent range. Likewise, a trace theorem on a smooth graph domain\nmust not be presented as the roadmap's full Lipschitz-domain theorem.\nPDEAtlas's hyperbolic equations, fluid constructions and CAS add further uses\nfor this shared analysis. We aim to contribute suitable general results to\nMathlib and coordinate contributions with Tau Ceti's maintainers and roadmap.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.1. Shared analysis","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Shared-analysis"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Null-Lagrangians":{"contents":"For a density  of a map , with\n standing for  and  for , the Euler–Lagrange expression in\ncomponent  is\n\n\n\nUsually this expression vanishes only for the critical points of the action\n. A null Lagrangian is a density for which it\nvanishes for every map.\n\nA density \nis a null Lagrangian on  if every smooth map  on \nsatisfies the Euler–Lagrange system, with classical derivatives, at every\npoint of . The determinant density is  on square\nmatrices.\n\nIn every dimension , the Euler–Lagrange expression of the determinant\nvanishes at every point where  is . In\nparticular the determinant is a null Lagrangian on every open set.\n\nThe derivative of  in the entry  is the cofactor\n, at singular matrices as well, and the density\ndoes not depend on . The Euler–Lagrange expression is therefore\n, and the rows of the cofactor\nmatrix of a  map are divergence free. This is the Piola identity. Each\ncofactor is a sum of products of first derivatives, and after\ndifferentiation the mixed second derivatives cancel in pairs.\n\nThe name is explained by what the action does. If the Euler–Lagrange\nexpression vanishes identically, the derivative of the action along any path\nof maps with fixed boundary values is zero, so the action depends only on the\nboundary values.\n\nLet  be a null Lagrangian on an open set  with  joint\ndependence on its arguments. Then every  map on  also\nsatisfies its Euler–Lagrange system. If  is bounded with \ngraph boundary and  has a continuous second jet, then two maps that are\n up to the boundary, continuous on , and equal on\n have the same action\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange expression at a point depends only on the second jet of\nthe map there, and every second jet is realized by a polynomial map, which is\nsmooth. This passes the null property from smooth maps to  maps.\nFor the action, extend  and  across the boundary to  maps on\nthe whole space, using reflection in the graph charts, and consider\n. The first-variation identity writes\n as the integral of the\nEuler–Lagrange expression of  against , plus a boundary term that\nvanishes because  on . The Euler–Lagrange expression\nis zero, so the action is constant in .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Null Lagrangians and Constraints","header":"9.3.3.1. Null Lagrangians","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Null-Lagrangians"}});